:: HelpWithCooking [childhood]
# Learning to Cook

You move closer to the cooking fire, where several women are preparing the day's meal. One of them, an aunt in your extended family, invites you to help.

"Here," she says, handing you a smooth stick. "Stir this pot of root stew so it doesn't burn at the bottom."

You carefully stir the thick mixture, which contains various roots, herbs, and small pieces of meat. The steam carries a rich, savory smell that makes your stomach rumble.

Your aunt explains how different foods need different cooking methods - some are roasted directly in the coals, others are boiled in water, and some are dried to preserve them for later use.

"Cooking makes many plants safer to eat," she tells you. "And it makes meat last longer before it spoils."

As you help, you learn not just about cooking, but about how fire itself is controlled - how to adjust the heat by moving food closer to or further from the flames.

